# BRO-2493: sync-audit-checkout.sh's union-recovery stage (BRO-2314) only
# unblocks a launchd job's `git merge --ff-only` for a tracked *.jsonl path
# declared merge=union here — 17 of the 20 tracked data/audit/*.jsonl ledgers
# were missing this attribute, which is the identical six-day-outage shape
# BRO-2314 just closed for the two files below, latent on every one of the
# 17. Each entry below was checked against its ACTUAL writer AND reader for
# the two things that disqualify a file from union:
#   (a) a line's disappearance is semantically load-bearing (a reader sums or
#       otherwise aggregates duplicate keys, or a resolved/consumed row must
#       not come back) — NOT merely "the writer prunes old rows"
#       (scraper-spend-ledger.jsonl above already establishes that a pure
#       chronological ring-buffer trim is an accepted, harmless union risk: a
#       union-resurrected pruned row is stale data, never a wrong computed
#       value);
#   (b) a reader assumes array/file order encodes true chronological order
#       (first-element-is-earliest, walk-newest-to-oldest, a consecutive-
#       streak count) WITHOUT re-sorting by an explicit timestamp field first
#       — sync-audit-checkout.sh's recovery stage appends the locally-saved
#       rows after origin's committed rows regardless of their real
#       timestamps (scripts/lib/sync-audit-decision.js's unionLedgerLines()),
#       so file order is NOT chronological order post-recovery. A reader that
#       explicitly re-sorts before using order (see remediation-log.jsonl's
#       readAttempts()/readGiveUps() below) is unaffected.
# Files that failed either check are deliberately NOT listed here — see the
# EXEMPT_LEDGERS array in scripts/lib/audit-ledger-merge-attrs.js, which a
# colocated CI gate cross-checks against this file so an 18th ledger can't
# arrive uncovered by either list.
